In https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/CELEBORN-2313 We have extended the checked zone by moving the mapper side CRC calculation earlier to Batch Assembly point, it could now protect us bugs that could arise from DataPusher/PushTask related logic. 

 

But there is still one class of correctness bugs that are not covered by E2E Check mechanism. E2E Check mechanism work base on one assumption: the mapper side CRC is trust worthy, but it is not necessarily true all the times. Take a look at https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/CELEBORN-1544,  E2E check can not detect it because mapper side CRC is wrong in this case. The close() method calls mapperEnd() no matter the write is success or not:

 

```

public void write(...) {
    try

   \{         // process records     }

catch (Exception e)

{         throw e;     }

finally

{         close(); // → triggers mapperEnd() with partial data!     }

}

```

 

So I propose to check the input iterator must be fully consumed before sending MapperEnd event: if there is any reason that causes input iterator not fully consumed, we will fail the mapper task instead of sending faulty MapperEnd event to Driver, give Celeborn another layer of protection.

