for (int i in 1..4) {
    println i ** 2
}
{code}
which might have this output:
{noformat}
1
16
9
4
{noformat}
Or these:
{code}
int sum = 0
@Invariant({ 0 <= i && i <= 4 })
for (int i in 0..4) {
    sum += i
}
assert sum == 10
{code}

{code}
int i = 10
@Invariant({ i >= 0 })
@Ensures({ i > old.i }) // i decreases
while (i > 0) {
    i = i - 1
}
assert i == 0
{code}

Consider the following:
{code:groovy}
interface I {
  def foo = new Object() {}
}
{code}

The anon. inner class I$1 is static because it is within an interface.  However, JLS 15.9.5 states "An anonymous class is always an inner class".  And JLS 8.1.3 further states "An inner class is a nested class that is not explicitly or implicitly static."

