h3. Statements sequence

 USE test;

 CREATE TYPE MovieType AS open {

    id: int,

    title: string,

    release_date: string

  };

 

 CREATE DATASET Movies(MovieType) PRIMARY KEY id;

 // Load dataset…

CREATE VECTOR INDEX movies10kVtree ON movies10K(embedding VECTOR)INCLUDE (release date) WITH
{"dimension":384,
 "train_list_number":1000,
 "num clusters":100，
 "quantization":"S8",
 "similarity":"dot",
};

*ERROR: Code: 1 "ASX1079: Compilation error: Cannot enforce field 'release_date' to have type union(string, null, missing)"*

Root Cause: The VectorIndexDetails is created with overrideKeyFieldTypes = true (hardcoded at line 1685 of QueryTranslator.java). This causes createEnforcedType to process it, which calls appendVectorIndexType, which calls enforcedTypeBuilder.build() for each include field. The problem is at line 314 of TypeUtil.java — when the field already exists in a closed schema (like release_date: string), the builder tries to "enforce" the type but the include field's type has been wrapped as union(string, null, missing) which can't be promoted from the original string. In short, Vector index INCLUDE fields were going through B-tree-style type enforcement. When release_date exists in a closed schema as string, the validation loop wraps it as union(string, null, missing). The enforced type builder then tries to enforce that union type against the existing string type.
Fix: vector index include fields that already exist in the schema don't need type enforcement. They're just being stored for retrieval, not for type casting.

CREATE VECTOR INDEX ix ON Dataset(embedding VECTOR) WITH { 

       "dimension":384,

       "train_list_number":100000,  /{*}/ if dataset doesn’t have this much records.{*}

       "quantization":"SQ8",

       "num_clusters":153,

       "similarity":"dot"

};


